## RESOLVED — suite 29/29, `nix build` green

Two independent bugs, both found by reading real artifacts rather than
reconstructing them.

### 1. Empty directories were lost by any tree round trip

Where the empty dir came from: `build-builtins.sh` MADE it, at the object layer
(`git mktree </dev/null` plus a hand-spliced entry), for the runner image's 1777
`/tmp` — a worktree cannot hold one, so the script went around the index. It does
not survive materialize -> read -> re-put, so deep-deps dropped it and the
worker-deepened `rustc` stopped matching bootstrap's hand-deepen (`2b142170` vs
`fd5f4c63`). `/tmp` now carries `.caos-keep` and the two agree byte-for-byte
(`499a719b`). Nothing anywhere has to special-case an empty directory now.

### 2. The client could not PUSH what the server already had

The 7 rustc-tool failures (`fatal: bad tree object <runner layer00>`) were NOT
that bug — fixing it changed nothing. The real chain, each step read off a
`tests/deps-dump` run inside a real wrapper:

- the wrapper DOES contain the object (`rgrep/DEEP-DEPS/rustc/DEEP-DEPS/runner/
  layer00` == the "missing" hash) — delivery was never wrong;
- the inner SERVER has it, and advertises `refs/caos/std` covering it;
- the inner CLIENT does not — and git can only mark an advertised tip
  uninteresting by traversing it LOCALLY, so the push dies on the first
  unreadable object.

A client never fetches the std tree (`std_tree` says so deliberately — a tree
fetch pulls the whole closure). Evaluation fetches only what it WALKS, and
nothing walks into `runner/layer00`. But an arg tree carries `std` whole, and a
rustc-built tool's image IS the runner delta — so only those tools tripped it.

Fix: `tests/lib/run-test.sh` points the client repo's
`objects/info/alternates` at `/tmp/seed-git/objects`, the repo the interpreter
already fetched the wrapper's std into. No bytes move, and it stays honest about
the subset — the seed repo holds exactly what the test declared.

`tests/deps-dump` is kept: a diagnostic test that asserts nothing and prints
what the harness delivered plus what the inner stack knows about it. It is what
broke this open after three wrong theories.

## Lesson worth keeping: verify the REAL artifact

Three failed theories in a row all came from reconstructing the deepened tree
from the HOST's std (`caos-cli get <std> && run /cas/std/deep-deps`), while a
wrapper is built from the TREE UNDER TEST's std — different builds, different
runner deltas. The reconstruction only ever agreed with itself. `/cas/std` seen
from inside a test container is the OUTER job's std, not the inner one, for the
same reason. When a delivery chain is suspect, dump the artifact.

## Gotcha worth keeping

`nix build` with TWO installables and TWO `-o` flags
(`nix build .#a -o result-a .#b -o result-b`) does NOT create both symlinks —
one is left pointing at a previous build, and the other can be missing entirely.
That cost an hour of chasing "stale" publishes that were really a stale symlink.
Build one output per invocation. (`--refresh` is NOT needed: nix picks up
dirty-tree edits fine — verified directly.)
